module Darklang.Cli.Docs.ForAIParts

// The reference sections of `docs for-ai`. Each is one part,
// `docs for-ai <part>`, so the first read stays short.

/// `docs for-ai packages`: saving declarations and changing them.
let packages(): String =
  """# Darklang AI: packages

## Authoring

A name is Owner.name, or Owner.Module.name to put it in a module. Use an owner
of your own, such as `Me`: `Darklang` is reserved for the standard library and
refuses a save.

Create several declarations atomically:

  module /Me.Example - <<'EOF'
  type State = { count: Int64 }
  let double(n: Int64): Int64 = n * 2
  let main(n: Int64): Int64 = double(n)
  EOF

For one function, stdin accepts a complete declaration; the shorter signature
and body fragment remains valid:

  fn /Me.Example.double - <<'EOF'
  let double(n: Int64): Int64 = n * 2
  EOF

At module scope values use `val name = value`; `let` declares functions. Inside
a function, local values use `let`. Nested helpers annotate every parameter
(the return type is optional; neither is checked): `let helper(x: Int64): Int64 = ...`.

`fn`, `type`, `val` and `module` run the at-rest type checker on what they save
and print its findings; the save goes through regardless (WIP is yours to break).
Updating existing items also re-checks their visible transitive dependents after
all propagation finishes and lists any that have definite type errors. Reports
count package locations rather than shared content hashes and do not misattribute
errors that already existed.
`commit` re-checks what it is committing and refuses a definite type error;
`commit --allow-type-errors` overrides that. An incomplete proof is reported but
never blocks: it means the checker lacked evidence, not that the declaration is
wrong. `typecheck` summarizes the whole branch, the standard library
included, so name your owner or module (`typecheck Me`) to see only yours;
`--all` also lists successful items, `--failed` or `--incomplete` lists only
that verdict.

## Changing something that exists

Read it, transform it, write it back. Do not retype a declaration to change one
line of it:

  view Some.fn --raw > /tmp/f.dark  # the declaration alone, nothing around it
                                    # ...edit /tmp/f.dark...
  edit Some.fn /tmp/f.dark          # applies it; no editor, no terminal needed

`--raw` prints exactly what `edit` and `module` accept, so this is a pipe rather
than a screen-scrape. Plain `view` is for a person to read and carries a
capabilities line you would have to strip. `edit <name>` with no file opens
$EDITOR instead, which is the shape for a human, not for you. And `--raw` is
the stored NORMAL FORM, not your keystrokes: formatting, redundant parens and
annotated nested lets come back normalized, so diff against a fresh `--raw`,
never against what you typed.

## A command-line tool

A function taking `args: List<String>` and returning `Int64` runs from the
shell by name. The words after the name are its arguments, as strings, and
the number it returns is the exit code:

  fn /Me.Greet.main - <<'EOF'
  let main(args: List<String>): Int64 =
    match args with
    | [ name ] ->
      Stdlib.printLine($"Hello, {name}")
      0
    | _ ->
      Stdlib.printErrorLine("usage: Me.Greet.main <name>")
      2
  EOF

  run Me.Greet.main Ada               # Hello, Ada; exits 0
  run Me.Greet.main                   # the usage line on stderr; exits 2

Printing needs no approval. Files, the network and the environment do:
`permissions approve Me.Greet.main --yes` remembers access for this version.
After an edit, `run` runs the edited code, which is denied that access until
`permissions update Me.Greet.main --yes`.

Numbers arrive as strings too: parse them with `Stdlib.Int64.parse`. Only a
`List<String>` parameter, or none, runs this way. A function with typed
parameters, such as `add(a: Int64, b: Int64)`, goes through `eval`:
`eval 'Me.Math.add(1, 2)'`.
"""


/// `docs for-ai tests`: package tests in full.
let tests(): String =
  """# Darklang AI: tests

## Package tests

Tests are ordinary Dark declarations stored separately from production
functions. They must return `Stdlib.Test.Result`:

  module /Me.Example.Tests - <<'EOF'
  test doubles =
    Me.Example.double(4)
    |> Stdlib.Test.equal(8)
  EOF

One test at a time, quoting the body so the shell leaves `|>` alone:

  test add Me.Example.Tests.doubles 'Me.Example.double(4) |> Stdlib.Test.equal(8)'

Run all tests with `test run`, or filter by name/module with `test run Example`.
`search` leaves tests and `Tests` modules out; add `--include-tests`, or `--test`
for tests only.
Tests use an allow-all instance policy for the test run and its disposable
workers; the installation's saved policy is unchanged. Run restrictions still
apply. Tests have no permission rows or grants of their own. A passing
ordinary test is reused when its calls are proven safe to reuse, on the same
branch with the same `Stdlib.Test.Result` binding, until the test body, one of
its followed dependencies, the runtime build, or the judging code changes.
Failed tests and expected-error tests rerun; so do tests with incomplete
cache-safety analysis.
`equal` and `notEqual` use branch-dependent formatting and rerun conservatively;
use `expect` with a fixed message for checks that can be cached.
`test run --force` reruns cached tests.

Every test execution runs in its own disposable process with an isolated store.
Ordinary `test name = ...` declarations provide isolation automatically, including
for tests that change the local store, branches, or environment. Safe cached
passes can still be reused. One starting snapshot per `test run` command is
shared read-only; a test that writes to it reruns on a private copy and is marked
`(reran on a copy)`, so anything it does outside its own home and temporary
directory happens twice. A command answered entirely from cache creates no
snapshot. Nested workers start from their calling test's current store.
Use `Stdlib.Test.Process.run` only when a test needs custom process options or
must inspect a child's output, result, or exit status.

To count evaluations or callbacks, use `Stdlib.Test.incrementCounter(value)` (returns
the value unchanged) and `Stdlib.Test.counterValue()`. The counter is
in memory and starts at zero for each test execution, including reruns.
These helpers work only in package tests, need no host permissions, and make
the test rerun rather than reuse a cached pass.

A test that expects a runtime error declares the expected outcome after its
body; the body may be any expression and passes only when the rendered message
matches exactly:

  test divisionByZero =
    (1 / 0)
    => raises "Cannot divide by 0"
"""


/// `docs for-ai permissions`: the instance policy and package approvals.
let permissions(): String =
  """# Darklang AI: permissions

Host permissions are managed with the `permissions` command (alias `perms`).
Instance rules supply default grants and explicit denies. Named approvals
can supply additional access; explicit denies and run restrictions always win. A fresh
installation permits package/local-store access, stdio, clock, and randomness;
rules allow (or deny) additional effects and resources:

  permissions list
  permissions profile                         # list starting policies
  permissions profile read-only /home/me/project       # preview: reads, HTTPS GET
  permissions profile local-dev /home/me/project --yes # apply: adds writes, serving
  permissions allow clock
  permissions allow http GET https://api.stripe.com/v1/*
  permissions allow file write /home/me/project/data   # absolute; ~ not expanded
  permissions deny http POST https://api.stripe.com/v1
  permissions allow native             # raw fds, process handles: cannot be scoped,
                                       # so it is granted whole or not at all
  permissions allow http-server 127.0.0.1 9090  # local serving only
  effects <fn>                        # possible effects, call paths, completeness
  permissions show <fn|hash>           # the function's effects and remembered approvals

## Agent runners

When Dark denies access, submit the exact `permissions allow` command from the
error through your runner's approval flow. Run it separately from the task,
without asking in chat first.
Ask me requests your approval; Approve for me uses the runner's reviewer;
bypass/dangerous mode skips review.

In Codex, request escalation even when the policy path is writable. In Claude,
use normal Bash review without allow or ask rules for permission commands.

Grants persist for all code in the instance. If review is denied or unavailable,
stop; do not bypass it. After the command succeeds, retry only if doing so will
not repeat earlier side effects. This guidance relies on runner review; it
does not prevent direct policy edits.

## Running code

Interactive `run` asks: Allow this run / Remember for this code version / Cancel.
Access Dark can see before execution is reviewed in one prompt; other access
pauses before its operation with a prompt of its own, so a run can ask more
than once. Approval continues without restarting earlier work.
Unattended runs fail with guidance; use `run --allow` for temporary access.
Remembered script approvals appear as `script:/absolute/path` in `permissions approved`.
Use that name with `permissions show` or `unapprove`.

Consumer approvals are local to this installation, like the instance policy,
with one approved version per logical function name. The
normal workflow uses the logical function name; immutable hashes stay behind
the approval:

  permissions approve Vendor.Package.function --yes
  permissions update Vendor.Package.function --yes
  permissions approved                  # each name and its approved version
  permissions unapprove Vendor.Package.function

Approval computes the function's inferred transitive needs; changing the code
changes its hash and requires a new decision, without changing the user's
logical name. Running a name uses its current code on the selected branch.
Edits to the function or its dependencies do not inherit the old approval.
Use `permissions update` or `approve` to review access for the new code.
Instance defaults and temporary run permissions still apply.
`unapprove` removes that name's remembered access without changing its code.
`revoke-package <hash>` removes all named approvals for that version.
"""


/// `docs for-ai traces`: reading a run back, and things that run at once.
let traces(): String =
  """# Darklang AI: traces

## Looking at what your code did

**You do not need any of this for something small.** Write it, run it, read the
output. Every command below costs a step, and a step you did not need is slower
than the print statement you were going to write anyway.

Reach for it when the thing you are building is slow to run, or involved enough
that you cannot hold what it did in your head: a handler with several calls in
it, something that talks to an API, anything you have run more than twice.

The loop, then:

  dark traces record on            keep a record of every run (off by default)
  dark run thing.dark              ... so this run is kept, with what it did
  dark traces                      the runs, newest first
  dark traces show <fn>            that function's code, with the values from
                                   the newest run that went through it

`traces show` is the one that replaces a print statement. It replays the run
with every impure call answered from what was recorded, so nothing is performed
a second time -- no second charge, no second write -- and the pure code is
recomputed, so the values are the values of the code as it is NOW. Edit a
callee, run it again, and the numbers move without re-running the world.

A line inside a loop carries ONE value, the last time round. To see another
iteration, name the loop:

  dark traces show <fn> --iteration map:3          that loop's third iteration
  dark traces show <fn> --iteration map:3,fold:3   two loops, same iteration

Names come from the code: `map` and `fold` are the function the lambda was
passed to. Two of a name are `map` and `map#2`. The footer says which iteration
each loop is showing. To read a function the run CALLED, just view it:
`dark traces show <that fn>`.

**For you, do not navigate.** `dark traces show <fn> --json` returns every loop,
every iteration and all of their values in ONE call, each carrying the
`iteration` string a person would type. A person goes a step at a time because several
answers to one question on one screen is confusing; you have no such problem and
a round trip costs you more than the bytes do.

Discovery goes both ways. `dark view <fn>` says how many traces went through a
function, and so does `dark edit <fn>` once the edit lands; `dark traces calls
<fn>` lists them; `dark traces inspect <id>` says which functions a trace ran, so
you can go from a run back to the code.

When a run stopped partway, `traces resume <id>` carries on from where it got
to rather than starting over, and `traces fork <id> --at <n>` runs a different
tail from the same recorded beginning: three variations cost one real run.

`traces calls` and `traces inspect` take `--json`, and so does `ps`. Use it:
the tables are laid out for a person and their columns will change. `resume`
and `fork` report in prose; they do work rather than answer a question.

Exit codes are the other thing you can branch on without reading English.
`eval` and `run` exit 0 when the code ran and gave an answer, and non-zero
when it raised, when the script was not there, or when the script itself
ended non-zero. A permission refusal is non-zero too.

## Live, and processes

Running things follow your edits WHEN ASKED. `serve <router> --live` runs the
newest router that passes its checks on every request; a broken save keeps the
previous one serving. `ps --watch` and `traces show <fn> --watch` are live
views over the same host loop. The workbench shows `// = value` beside calls
from the last recorded run.

Every run is a process: reads run at once with no code change, writes keep order
(overlap them with `List.parallelMap` or `Exec.spawn`/`await`), `Stdlib.await(x)`
waits here; `Exec.spawn`/`await`/`cancel` when you want control;
`ps` shows what is running on the machine, `traces resume <id>` picks a Ctrl-C'd
run back up, `traces fork` branches one. `docs processes` and `docs live` have the
short versions; `docs/processes.md` and `docs/live.md` in the repo have the design.
"""


/// `docs for-ai scm`: drafts, commits, branches and deprecation.
let scm(): String =
  """# Darklang AI: scm

## SCM (version control)

Your edits are LIVE the moment you author them. `commit` doesn't make them
real -- it reviews them and records them. Before that they're your draft: running,
changeable, not yet anyone else's.

Editing an item repoints all its callers immediately, across owners. That's
PROPAGATION, and it's the one behaviour to know about, because it means a
`status` after one edit can show two changed items.

  status [--json]      what changed, what FOLLOWED, what's waiting
  commit <msg> [-y]    review + commit; -y skips the prompt
  commit --json        a dry review -- exactly what committing would do
  discard [-y]         drop the whole draft
  undo <name>          step ONE item back one version
  deps <name> --json   what it uses and what uses it, each flagged `behind`
  ack --all            leave every constraint finding for now

Every question-answering command takes `--json` (status, commit's dry review,
conflicts, constraints, branches, diff, deps, search, typecheck), and it's the
same data the human view renders. `status --json` flags each changed item
`followed` or not, which is how you tell what you did from what propagation did.
Authoring commands exit nonzero when nothing was saved (a parse error, a name
mismatch), so branch on the exit, not on grepping for "error".
  (see: docs scm -- the full model, branches, merge, conflicts)

## Branches

A branch is its own layer of names over its parent's, in the same store, invisible to main until it merges, and it sees only COMMITTED work from main and its parents -- a draft is private to where it was authored.
Several agents on several branches is the case this is built for: one process
each, and they never see each other's work until someone merges.

  dark switch feature              # persists; the next `dark` starts here
  dark --branch feature <cmd>      # ONE command, without switching
  dark branches [--json]           # what exists
  dark diff feature                # what it changes vs its parent
  dark rebase feature              # take the parent's current state
  dark merge feature -y            # fold it in (-y: nothing is there to answer the prompt)

Conflicts never block. Both sides converge on the same winner, the loser is
kept, and `dark conflicts` is where you review it after.
  (see: docs scm)

## Your history

  dark commits                     # THIS branch's commits, newest first
  dark ops                         # the op log itself
  dark show <hash>                 # what one commit contains, or what one op does

Ops are content-addressed and the log is append-only, so nothing you have
committed can be rewritten, only superseded.
  (see: docs scm)

## Deprecation (mark items as unused / dangerous / obsolete)
  deprecate <fn|type|value> <name> --kind <k> [options]
    kinds: superseded-by (requires --replacement), harmful, obsolete
  delete <fn|type|value> <name> [options]     # sugar for --kind obsolete
    options: --ignore-dependents; --force = --ignore-dependents + --yes
  deprecate/delete ... --json                 # one object; without --yes, a dry run
  undeprecate <name>                          # clear it, any kind, harmful included
  eval --allow-harmful <fn>                   # opt out of harmful halt
  (see: docs deprecation)
"""


/// `docs for-ai syntax`: the full syntax notes and name resolution.
let syntax(): String =
  """# Darklang AI: syntax

## Names and syntax

- `Stdlib.X` expands to `Darklang.Stdlib.X`. Cross-owner names need a full path.
- Never write `PACKAGE.` in source or search; it is internal notation.
- A package item named `__name` is an internal helper, not public API. `ls`,
  `tree`, `search`, `docs` and completion hide it (`--include-internal` shows
  it); `view`, calls and `deps` reach it by its full name.
- Lists use commas: `[1, 2]`; chars use single quotes: `'a'`.
- `|>` passes the value on its left in as the first argument. If that value is
  an `if` or `match`, put it in parentheses, or only its last branch gets piped.
- Strings concatenate with `+`. Interpolation uses the type's `Display` impl and
  falls back to `Stdlib.inspect(x)`, so `$"{x}"` works for any value.
- `Stdlib.display(x)` needs a `Display` impl (there is no `Stdlib.Int64.toString`);
  lists, tuples and Unit have none. `Stdlib.inspect(x)` shows any value. Any trait
  method is called through the trait, not the type: `Stdlib.Add.add(1, 2)`, or
  receiver first, `x.display`.
  `view Stdlib.Int64` lists the type's impls with where each method is called.
- A bare integer is an `Int64` and a bare `1.5` a `Float64`; numeric operands must
  have one type. Other number types take a suffix (table in Critical Syntax).
  As a type, `Int` means `Int64` and `Float` means `Float64`.
- Local values use `let max = 6` without annotations; package values use `val`.
- A keyword (type, match, in, ...; `docs syntax` lists them) is a name in
  backticks: let `type` = "json". After a `.` and as a record field name it
  needs none: r.type, Metadata { type = "json" }.
- Record construction is `State { count = 0 }`; update is `{ state with count = 1 }`.
- `f(x)?` extracts Ok/Some or immediately returns Error/None from the
  nearest function/lambda. Return Result/Option and still write Ok/Some on
  success; Result error types must agree.
- Option/Result cases work bare: `Some(x)`, `None`, `Ok(x)`, `Error(e)`.
- A constructor's fields go in parentheses, as a call's arguments do:
  `Pair(1, 2)`, `| Ok(x) ->`. `Ok x` is a parse error.
- User enum construction uses `TypeName.Case`; match cases may be bare or
  qualified (`| Result.Ok(x)`).
- A tuple payload needs double parentheses: `Some((a, b))`.
- Lambda parameters take the call shape: `fun x ->`, `fun(acc, x) ->`; one
  tuple parameter is `fun((a, b)) ->`.
- A function type with several arguments is `(A, B) -> C`; `A -> B` takes one.

## Critical Syntax
- Whitespace-sensitive (like Python)
- Calls put the arguments in parentheses: `Stdlib.String.length(s)`,
  `Stdlib.List.map(xs, fun x -> x + 1)`. `f()` calls a fn declared `let f(): T`.
  `f a b` is a parse error.
- No partial application: a call gives every argument, and too few is an error,
  not a function. To supply the rest later, write a lambda: `fun x -> Stdlib.max(1, x)`.
- Declarations list their parameters the same way: `let add(a: Int64, b: Int64): Int64 = a + b`.
- Nested function definitions need PARENTHESIZED, annotated params:
  `let helper(x: Int64): Int64 = ...`. The return type is optional, and neither
  annotation is checked. The parser desugars this to a local lambda, so
  use it for task-local helpers. A nested fn can call one defined above it; a FORWARD
  reference to one defined below fails, and so does mutual recursion.
- NO binding modules to variables (`let c = Darklang.Cli.Colors` is invalid --
  modules are not first-class values. Use the full path each time.)
- Pipe |> feeds the left value as the FIRST argument of the right fn; any
  args in its parentheses come after it: `[1, 2] |> Stdlib.List.map(toString)`
  is `Stdlib.List.map([1, 2], toString)`, and `x |> f()` is `f(x)`. You can't
  pipe straight into an operator (`n |> + 1` won't parse), so write `n + 1`, or
  use a lambda mid-pipe: `|> (fun v -> v + 1)`. If the left side is an `if` or
  `match`, wrap it in parentheses: `(if c then a else b) |> fn`.
- Every line of a block except the last must return Unit; drop any other value with
  `let _ = f(x)`. `run` and `eval` type-check code with more than one statement first, and
  run none of it when a line drops a value or has another definite type error, even in a
  branch that is not taken.
- Lists: [1, 2, 3] (commas; a semicolon is a parse error)
- Spread joins lists: `[x, ...xs]`, `[...a, ...b]`. There is no `::` or `@`.
- Dicts and records use commas or newlines, never semicolons:
  `Dict { "a": 1, "b": 2 }`, `Point { x = 1, y = 2 }`,
  `type Point = { x: Int64, y: Int64 }`, `{ point with x = 3, y = 4 }`.
- Sets: `Set { 1, 2, 3 }`, type `Set<Int64>`, ops in `Stdlib.Set` (`contains`,
  `add`, `remove`, `union`, `intersection`, `difference`, `isSubset`, `map`,
  `filter`, `fold`). A repeat is kept once; there are no set patterns.
- Char literals use single quotes: 'a', '\n', '\t', 'A' (a String uses
  double quotes; "a" is not 'a')
- Tuples destructure inline in `let` and in lambda params:
  `let (a, b) = pair`, `fun((name, done)) -> ...`
- Tuple elements by position, counting from 1: `pair.1`, `triple.3`,
  `nested.1.2`. `.0` and past-the-end are errors.
- String concat: +
- String interpolation: $"x = {expr}; total = {a + b}" -- requires `ToString`;
  its implementation is resolved and recorded when saved.
- Most operators are traits: `+ - * / % **` and `-x` are Add Subtract Multiply Divide Modulo
  Power Negate; `< <= > >=` are Compare; `& | ^ << >>` and `~x` are BitwiseAnd BitwiseOr
  BitwiseXor ShiftLeft ShiftRight BitwiseNot. `==` and `!=` are NOT traits: structural on
  every value, always, and no type can override them.
  Operands must match (`1 + 2`, `1.0 + 2.0`; `1 + 2.0` is an error). `/` is
  integer division on ints, float on floats; `**` is power; a bare `1` is an
  `Int64`, which wraps on overflow. `"a" + "b"` is `"ab"`.
  `19.99dec` is a `Decimal`: exact base-10, the type for money; `Stdlib.Decimal.round`,
  `roundWith` (a `RoundingMode`), `floor`, `ceiling`, `power`, `parse`, `toParts`.
- Number literals. The suffix decides the type; nothing infers it:
    1, 1s, 1s64   Int64          1u, 1u64   UInt64
    1s8 1s16 1s32 1s128          1u8 1u16 1u32 1u128
    1z            BigInt         1.5, 1e5, 1.5f64   Float64
    1.5f32        Float32        19.99dec   Decimal (no exponent)
  `z` is for ℤ, the integers: `1z` is a BigInt, with no size limit.
  Values print with their suffix except Int64 and Float64, so output pastes back as itself.
- Traits: `impl Add for Vec = let add(a: Vec, b: Vec): Vec = ...` gives Vec
  a `+`; `impl Display for Point = let display(p: Point): String = ...`
  gives `p.display`; your own with `trait Area<'a> = let area(s: 'a): Float64`.
  A bound is `let f<'a: Display>(v: 'a) = ...`. Author with `dark trait` /
  `dark impl`; `dark impls <Trait> --json` lists implementations with the status a
  call sees (active, shadowed, unordered); `dark constraints --json` has the rival
  pairs; `docs traits`.
- Bitwise operators have implementations for every integer type and none for
  Float64 or Float32; both operands (the shift count included) must be the same type.
  The count is taken modulo the bit width, as in Rust: `1u8 << 9u8` is `2u8`.
- `!x` is boolean not. Inside a `match`, `|` is the arm separator, so bitwise-or
  there needs parens: `| A -> (a | b)`.

## Records
  MyRecord { a = 1, b = 2 }
  # { must not be left of type name

## Enums
  type Shape = | Dot | Seg(Point, Point)   # declare: fields in parentheses, like a call
  Option.Some(5)     # construct: TypeName.Case, fields in parentheses
  | Some(x) -> ...           # match: Case, or Type.Case
  | (Ok(x), Ok(y)) -> ...    # tuple of constructors
  | Some(Ok(v)) -> ...       # constructor of a constructor
  | Some((a, b)) -> ...      # constructor wrapping a tuple NEEDS its inner parens
  | [x, ...rest] -> ...      # head and rest of a list; `...rest` goes last

## Name Resolution
Names resolve relative to the current module, searching from
most-specific to least-specific.

In `module Darklang.Stdlib.List`, writing `Option.Option` tries:
  1. Darklang.Stdlib.List.Option.Option
  2. Darklang.Stdlib.Option.Option   <-- found here
  3. Darklang.Option.Option
  4. Option.Option

Rules:
  - Same module: no qualifier needed
    (in Darklang.Stdlib.List, `map` finds Darklang.Stdlib.List.map)
  - Parent/sibling modules: use relative name
    (in Darklang.Stdlib.List, `Option.Option` finds
     Darklang.Stdlib.Option.Option)
  - `Stdlib.X` shortcut: always expands to `Darklang.Stdlib.X`
    (works from any module, even outside Darklang)
  - `Builtin.X`: resolves to F# builtins (fns/values only, not types)
  - Cross-owner: must use full path
    (from Tests.*, write `Darklang.SCM.PackageOps.currentBranch`)
"""
